1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,333
Eleven at night

2
00:00:01,333 --> 00:00:02,900
You open the job app again

3
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:04,133
Scroll through a few listings

4
00:00:04,133 --> 00:00:05,500
None of them feel like you

5
00:00:05,733 --> 00:00:07,266
You switch to a course page

6
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:08,800
Photography looks interesting

7
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:10,666
Coding seems to have a future too

8
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:12,666
Making content sounds free

9
00:00:12,866 --> 00:00:15,066
Learning a craft feels more solid

10
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:16,066
After all that scrolling

11
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,600
You end up clicking nothing

12
00:00:17,666 --> 00:00:19,066
Phone face down by the pillow

13
00:00:19,166 --> 00:00:20,566
One thought left in your head

14
00:00:20,666 --> 00:00:22,066
What do I actually like

15
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:23,966
That question traps a lot of people

16
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:26,366
It sounds like you're searching for direction

17
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:28,266
But sometimes what it's really asking is

18
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:32,000
Can someone tell me which road won't be wasted before I even start

19
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:35,300
We're used to thinking passion is an answer hidden somewhere

20
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:37,566
As if everyone has a lamp inside them

21
00:00:37,733 --> 00:00:38,966
And once you find the right switch

22
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,466
It just lights up

23
00:00:40,566 --> 00:00:42,700
So some people keep taking personality tests

24
00:00:42,866 --> 00:00:45,466
Some study every little interest so carefully

25
00:00:45,566 --> 00:00:48,100
Some wait for something to spark a rush of excitement

26
00:00:48,133 --> 00:00:49,966
No spark no starting

27
00:00:49,966 --> 00:00:52,666
But that's rarely how passion actually shows up

28
00:00:52,766 --> 00:00:54,466
The coffee you love drinking now

29
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:56,833
Probably didn't taste special the first time

30
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,766
The work you handle so smoothly now

31
00:00:58,866 --> 00:01:01,966
Started out as clumsy little tasks

32
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:03,600
That sport you fell for

33
00:01:03,666 --> 00:01:05,400
Probably wasn't fun the first time

34
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,366
It was after learning a bit that you felt in control

35
00:01:08,366 --> 00:01:10,433
A lot of what you love isn't discovered

36
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:13,100
It grows slowly after you've done it a few times

37
00:01:13,133 --> 00:01:15,766
That's not great news if you're in a hurry for an answer

38
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:16,800
Because it means

39
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:18,666
You have to let yourself stay unsure first

40
00:01:18,733 --> 00:01:21,466
You have to try things that don't look worth it yet

41
00:01:21,566 --> 00:01:23,800
You have to accept the first few tries won't be good

42
00:01:23,866 --> 00:01:25,100
But think about it

43
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:27,833
What really traps people usually isn't having no options

44
00:01:27,933 --> 00:01:31,800
It's having too many and every one feels like a test you can't fail

45
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:33,100
You're afraid learning it won't pay off

46
00:01:33,166 --> 00:01:35,566
Afraid switching paths makes you look unstable

47
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:38,033
Afraid you'll spend three months and end up not liking it

48
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:40,866
So the safest move seems to be just keep thinking

49
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:42,633
But endless thinking has a cost too

50
00:01:42,766 --> 00:01:45,200
You get better and better at judging other people's paths

51
00:01:45,266 --> 00:01:47,166
And end up with less and less proof of your own

52
00:01:47,266 --> 00:01:49,866
This is exactly where passion gets misunderstood

53
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:52,033
It's not a pass you're handed before you set off

54
00:01:52,166 --> 00:01:55,433
It's more like a receipt you get after walking part of the way

55
00:01:55,866 --> 00:01:58,166
Economics has a really useful distinction

56
00:01:58,466 --> 00:01:59,966
Called exploration and exploitation

57
00:02:00,366 --> 00:02:01,600
Exploitation is easy to get

58
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:04,033
You already know which restaurant is good

59
00:02:04,166 --> 00:02:06,100
So every time you're hungry you go back there

60
00:02:06,266 --> 00:02:07,233
It saves time

61
00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:08,500
And you rarely get burned

62
00:02:08,966 --> 00:02:10,033
Exploration is

63
00:02:10,166 --> 00:02:12,566
Occasionally trying some place you've never been

64
00:02:12,766 --> 00:02:13,800
You might get burned

65
00:02:13,866 --> 00:02:15,800
Or you might find a taste you love even more

66
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:17,200
If all you do is exploit

67
00:02:17,333 --> 00:02:18,566
Life stays pretty stable

68
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:22,433
But your choices only ever come from that small slice you already know

69
00:02:22,666 --> 00:02:23,966
If all you do is explore

70
00:02:24,066 --> 00:02:25,666
You keep switching from one thing to another

71
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:28,166
And it's hard to go deep on anything

72
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:29,966
Finding what you love works the same way

73
00:02:30,266 --> 00:02:32,233
A lot of people want to jump straight to exploiting

74
00:02:32,466 --> 00:02:35,000
They want to find the perfect career immediately

75
00:02:35,166 --> 00:02:38,566
With just the right pay growth freedom and meaning

76
00:02:38,733 --> 00:02:39,700
But here's the problem

77
00:02:39,766 --> 00:02:41,200
You haven't explored anything yet

78
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:43,233
You don't even have enough samples to work with

79
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:48,000
You're letting other people's videos resumes and feelings decide for you

80
00:02:48,266 --> 00:02:50,100
It's like never having tasted a dish

81
00:02:50,266 --> 00:02:53,066
And judging whether you'll like it just from the reviews

82
00:02:53,233 --> 00:02:54,033
It's hard

83
00:02:54,133 --> 00:02:57,566
Because liking something rarely comes from its name

84
00:02:57,666 --> 00:02:59,833
It comes from what it actually feels like to do it

85
00:02:59,966 --> 00:03:03,500
When you write do you hate researching or hate revising sentences

86
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:07,400
When you shoot video do you love hunting for footage or explaining something clearly

87
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:12,000
When you work with data do you enjoy finding patterns or making tidy spreadsheets

88
00:03:12,166 --> 00:03:14,800
None of these details show up until you actually do it once

89
00:03:14,966 --> 00:03:16,766
There's something else people often overlook

90
00:03:16,933 --> 00:03:17,900
It's called a sense of competence

91
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,700
The first time you do most things there's no passion at all

92
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:22,566
Because all you feel is clumsy

93
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:23,966
Your fingers hurt pressing guitar strings

94
00:03:24,133 --> 00:03:26,000
You can never find the rhythm editing video

95
00:03:26,066 --> 00:03:27,466
You put too much salt in the food

96
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:30,033
You stare at the first article unable to write an opening

97
00:03:30,266 --> 00:03:32,666
If all you ask yourself then is whether you like it

98
00:03:32,766 --> 00:03:34,400
The answer is usually no

99
00:03:34,533 --> 00:03:36,000
But once you do it a few more times

100
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:37,433
Your hands start to cooperate

101
00:03:37,533 --> 00:03:38,966
You can see a little progress

102
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,866
Only then are you qualified to know if you're actually interested

103
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:44,200
Not all persistence grows into passion

104
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:47,233
But without any repetition passion rarely grows at all

105
00:03:47,366 --> 00:03:49,200
Passion isn't talent speaking up

106
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:52,166
Most of the time it's feedback finally letting you hear yourself

107
00:03:52,666 --> 00:03:54,833
Picture a scene you know all too well

108
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:56,066
A class reunion

109
00:03:56,366 --> 00:03:57,700
Someone landed a job at a big company

110
00:03:57,900 --> 00:03:59,433
Someone got a stable government job

111
00:03:59,966 --> 00:04:01,366
Someone started their own business

112
00:04:01,666 --> 00:04:04,500
Listening to them everyone seems to have found their path

113
00:04:04,866 --> 00:04:06,033
When it's your turn to talk

114
00:04:06,166 --> 00:04:10,366
Suddenly any direction that's still uncertain feels embarrassing

115
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:13,266
So you don't dare say you just want to try something

116
00:04:13,366 --> 00:04:14,433
You're afraid someone will ask

117
00:04:14,566 --> 00:04:15,800
Can this make money

118
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:16,800
How long can you keep doing it

119
00:04:16,900 --> 00:04:18,266
What if it fails

120
00:04:18,566 --> 00:04:20,400
There's nothing wrong with these questions themselves

121
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:21,600
The problem is

122
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:24,300
We keep asking them way too early

123
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:26,600
You haven't even finished writing one full piece

124
00:04:26,666 --> 00:04:29,666
And you're already forcing yourself to answer if writing can pay the bills

125
00:04:30,066 --> 00:04:31,866
You haven't taken a single pottery class

126
00:04:31,966 --> 00:04:34,000
And you're already calculating the payoff three years out

127
00:04:34,266 --> 00:04:35,866
You've never led a single project

128
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:38,300
And you're already deciding if you're cut out for management

129
00:04:38,533 --> 00:04:39,666
That's not caution

130
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:42,700
That's using the final question to block the very first step

131
00:04:42,966 --> 00:04:46,400
There's another pressure too from all those picture-perfect lives online

132
00:04:46,666 --> 00:04:49,100
Someone knew they'd start a business at twenty

133
00:04:49,333 --> 00:04:51,600
Someone switched careers and got results right away

134
00:04:51,733 --> 00:04:54,400
Someone turned a hobby into a beautiful little channel

135
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:57,000
What you see is the highlight reel they edited

136
00:04:57,166 --> 00:04:59,200
Not the boring trial and error behind it

137
00:04:59,466 --> 00:05:00,500
So you start to assume

138
00:05:00,666 --> 00:05:03,666
That people who truly have direction never hesitated at all

139
00:05:03,933 --> 00:05:06,100
Actually most of them didn't start with a direction

140
00:05:06,166 --> 00:05:08,900
They started with something so small nobody noticed

141
00:05:09,133 --> 00:05:11,233
And the direction only surfaced as they kept going

142
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:14,400
The third trap is thinking passion means being happy forever

143
00:05:14,566 --> 00:05:17,666
Loving to draw doesn't mean you'll love endless revisions

144
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:21,566
Loving content creation doesn't mean you'll love the days the numbers tank

145
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:24,866
Loving to teach doesn't mean you'll love prepping lessons till midnight

146
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:28,000
A thing worth sticking with isn't one that never tires you out

147
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:30,300
It's one you're still willing to come back to after it does

148
00:05:30,566 --> 00:05:32,600
That's a far more reliable test

149
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:34,800
Not asking if you're excited the whole time

150
00:05:34,933 --> 00:05:38,066
But asking if you're willing to make it just a little better

151
00:05:38,366 --> 00:05:41,566
So don't treat one attempt like a life sentence

152
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:43,566
It's more like trying on a pair of shoes

153
00:05:43,766 --> 00:05:45,400
You have to walk a few steps in them first

154
00:05:45,466 --> 00:05:46,866
Before you know where they rub

155
00:05:46,966 --> 00:05:48,433
And where they can actually take you far

156
00:05:48,933 --> 00:05:51,500
If you still don't know what you like right now

157
00:05:51,666 --> 00:05:54,100
Stop forcing yourself to answer the huge question

158
00:05:54,533 --> 00:05:56,500
Swap it for a much smaller one

159
00:05:56,933 --> 00:05:58,800
Am I willing to give this two hours

160
00:05:58,800 --> 00:05:59,533
Not three years

161
00:05:59,533 --> 00:06:00,500
Not a lifetime

162
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:01,700
Just two hours

163
00:06:01,866 --> 00:06:02,833
Want to try writing

164
00:06:02,933 --> 00:06:05,200
Find a question that's genuinely been bugging you lately

165
00:06:05,466 --> 00:06:07,000
Write five hundred words and show a friend

166
00:06:07,133 --> 00:06:08,166
Want to try making video

167
00:06:08,333 --> 00:06:10,833
Grab your phone and explain something small you know well

168
00:06:10,933 --> 00:06:11,933
Want to try design

169
00:06:11,933 --> 00:06:14,766
Redesign one screen of an app you use all the time

170
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:15,866
Want to try cooking

171
00:06:15,866 --> 00:06:18,100
Carefully recreate a dish you love eating

172
00:06:18,166 --> 00:06:20,000
This little experiment needs three limits

173
00:06:20,166 --> 00:06:21,900
First don't spend too much money

174
00:06:21,966 --> 00:06:24,433
Don't buy a room full of gear the moment you're mildly curious

175
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:26,600
Second don't let it eat all your time

176
00:06:26,666 --> 00:06:29,700
Don't demand you do it every single night after work from day one

177
00:06:29,966 --> 00:06:32,400
Third leave behind something you can actually see

178
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:33,300
An article

179
00:06:33,300 --> 00:06:34,100
A piece of audio

180
00:06:34,233 --> 00:06:35,033
A dish

181
00:06:35,033 --> 00:06:35,833
A drawing

182
00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:37,800
Or an interview you actually carried out

183
00:06:37,966 --> 00:06:39,566
It doesn't have to go online

184
00:06:39,566 --> 00:06:41,166
It's evidence for yourself

185
00:06:41,166 --> 00:06:43,266
When you're done don't just ask if you liked it

186
00:06:43,266 --> 00:06:44,600
Write down three things

187
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:46,300
How much I resisted before starting

188
00:06:46,333 --> 00:06:48,400
Which part I was most absorbed in while doing it

189
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:50,433
Whether I'd be willing to do it again afterward

190
00:06:50,466 --> 00:06:51,600
And a fourth question

191
00:06:51,766 --> 00:06:54,600
If I did it again which step would I want to do a little better

192
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:55,866
That question matters a lot

193
00:06:55,866 --> 00:06:57,666
Because it helps you tell two kinds of tired apart

194
00:06:57,700 --> 00:07:00,900
One kind is finishing something and never wanting to touch it again

195
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:04,466
The other is feeling clumsy but already thinking how to improve next time

196
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:07,433
That second kind is usually more reliable than a brief burst of excitement

197
00:07:07,566 --> 00:07:08,966
It might not feel like passion

198
00:07:08,966 --> 00:07:10,566
It's more like a willingness to lean in

199
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:13,366
You don't have to give it some grand name right away

200
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:15,400
No need to ask if it's your calling

201
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,466
No rush to turn it into a side hustle either

202
00:07:17,566 --> 00:07:20,066
Just let it be one small thing done properly for now

203
00:07:20,133 --> 00:07:21,633
I have a friend who likes photography

204
00:07:21,733 --> 00:07:23,800
At first she always felt she had no eye for it

205
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:25,766
She didn't sign up for some expensive class right away

206
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:28,900
She just spent an hour shooting the same block for four weekends straight

207
00:07:29,133 --> 00:07:30,300
Week one she shot signs

208
00:07:30,300 --> 00:07:31,566
Week two windows

209
00:07:31,566 --> 00:07:33,233
Week three only strangers' hands

210
00:07:33,266 --> 00:07:35,500
Week four she went home and laid all the photos out together

211
00:07:35,533 --> 00:07:37,633
She still wouldn't say she's passionate about photography

212
00:07:37,666 --> 00:07:38,833
But she already knows

213
00:07:38,866 --> 00:07:40,700
She's willing to get up early for the light

214
00:07:40,733 --> 00:07:43,200
And willing to stare at one photo a few extra minutes

215
00:07:43,266 --> 00:07:44,333
That's the evidence

216
00:07:44,333 --> 00:07:46,200
Not someone praising your talent

217
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:49,166
It's noticing you keep bringing your own attention back here

218
00:07:49,166 --> 00:07:52,033
Or say someone wants to know if they're cut out for making content

219
00:07:52,100 --> 00:07:53,466
Don't stare at the view count first

220
00:07:53,533 --> 00:07:56,633
First try clearly explaining three questions you actually care about

221
00:07:56,900 --> 00:07:58,200
After you're done check

222
00:07:58,200 --> 00:07:59,633
Do you only care about the numbers

223
00:07:59,666 --> 00:08:02,800
Or does it feel good that someone finally got it

224
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:05,400
Different feedback pulls you in different directions

225
00:08:05,466 --> 00:08:08,300
You're not running this experiment to prove you're impressive

226
00:08:08,333 --> 00:08:10,800
You're using it to clear up how well you actually know yourself

227
00:08:10,866 --> 00:08:13,166
Of course these small experiments need some repetition too

228
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:14,600
Try writing something today

229
00:08:14,666 --> 00:08:16,266
Try five minutes of piano tomorrow

230
00:08:16,366 --> 00:08:17,800
Switch to baking the day after

231
00:08:17,866 --> 00:08:20,200
Hopping around like that won't give you a real read

232
00:08:20,300 --> 00:08:21,500
A better way is

233
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:22,666
Pick one direction

234
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:24,466
Give it three to five real tries

235
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:26,166
Changing just one small thing each time

236
00:08:26,166 --> 00:08:28,033
Then decide whether to keep going

237
00:08:28,133 --> 00:08:29,500
That's not delaying a decision

238
00:08:29,533 --> 00:08:32,300
It's trading a small cost now for a truer answer

239
00:08:32,466 --> 00:08:34,866
That gets you closer to yourself than any personality test

240
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:36,833
Because it's not the version of you that you imagine

241
00:08:36,966 --> 00:08:39,366
It's the reaction that's left after you actually did it

242
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:40,766
Some experiments will prove

243
00:08:40,766 --> 00:08:42,066
You genuinely don't like something

244
00:08:42,066 --> 00:08:43,233
That's not wasted either

245
00:08:43,333 --> 00:08:46,633
Ruling out a path that doesn't fit is a win for exploration too

246
00:08:46,666 --> 00:08:49,200
Some experiments won't get your blood pumping right away

247
00:08:49,266 --> 00:08:50,300
But you'll notice

248
00:08:50,333 --> 00:08:52,200
You're still willing to give it a bit more time

249
00:08:52,266 --> 00:08:53,133
And that's enough

250
00:08:53,133 --> 00:08:55,866
Passion doesn't have to strike like lightning in a movie

251
00:08:55,933 --> 00:08:58,166
It can start like a small flame just lit on the stove

252
00:08:58,233 --> 00:08:59,033
Small at first

253
00:08:59,066 --> 00:09:00,600
But you keep coming back to check on it

254
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:02,733
You don't have to find your life's answer right now

255
00:09:02,733 --> 00:09:05,400
You just need to stop waiting for an answer that requires no starting

256
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:08,066
Next time you scroll past someone's shiny career story

257
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:10,366
Don't rush to ask if you should be doing that too

258
00:09:10,366 --> 00:09:11,166
Ask yourself instead

259
00:09:11,266 --> 00:09:12,900
Can I give something a try this week

260
00:09:12,966 --> 00:09:14,466
Passion isn't something you find

261
00:09:14,466 --> 00:09:17,566
It's doing it again and again then revising and coming back

262
00:09:17,566 --> 00:09:19,100
Slowly recognizing your own shape
